1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,001
December 2008.

2
00:00:01,001 --> 00:00:05,646
A fishing boat slips out of a small harbor
on Korea's west coast.

3
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:11,864
According to investigators, one of the passengers is
the most wanted man in the country.

4
00:00:12,414 --> 00:00:18,908
Behind him, tens of thousands of people are
discovering that their savings are gone.

5
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:23,826
Retirees. Homemakers. Small shop owners.
Families who had been promised

6
00:00:23,826 --> 00:00:29,235
that a simple health machine would pay
them every single day.

7
00:00:30,235 --> 00:00:35,359
Three years later, the news arrived from
China. He was dead. Heart attack.

8
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:39,307
There was a death certificate. There was a
funeral video.

9
00:00:39,307 --> 00:00:41,384
And almost nobody believed it.

10
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:45,235
This is the story of Cho Hee-pal. How the
scheme worked,

11
00:00:45,235 --> 00:00:49,490
who let it run, and why a country still
argues about one funeral.

12
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:56,571
The story begins in Daegu, a big industrial
city in South Korea's southeast.

13
00:00:56,571 --> 00:00:59,518
Daegu is known as a conservative,

14
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:03,939
tight-knit place. Neighbors know
neighbors. Word of mouth matters.

15
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:06,618
That would turn out to be important.

16
00:01:07,168 --> 00:01:13,110
The man at the center of this story was
Cho Hee-pal, born in 1957.

17
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:18,845
Korean media, citing investigators, described him as someone
with only an elementary-school education,

18
00:01:18,845 --> 00:01:27,705
who reportedly worked as a laborer and drifted through small businesses, and
who learned the multi-level marketing trade in his forties.

19
00:01:27,955 --> 00:01:32,370
In October 2004, he set up a company in
Daegu called BMC.

20
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:37,094
And it started selling a deal that sounded
almost too simple.

21
00:01:37,644 --> 00:01:42,967
Korea is famous for its public bathhouses
and saunas, called jjimjilbang.

22
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:44,790
The pitch went like this.

23
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:49,534
You buy a health device. A massage
machine, a pelvic-correction bed,

24
00:01:49,534 --> 00:01:56,478
even a karaoke machine. One unit cost 4.4 million won,
roughly four thousand dollars at the time.

25
00:01:56,478 --> 00:02:02,337
You never take it home. The company rents your
machine out to bathhouses and clinics.

26
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:04,368
And you get paid every day.

27
00:02:04,918 --> 00:02:09,411
Let's walk through it the way an investor
would have seen it.

28
00:02:09,411 --> 00:02:13,388
Step one. You pay 4.4 million won. That's
one machine.

29
00:02:13,938 --> 00:02:18,185
Step two. According to Korean reporting
from the time,

30
00:02:18,185 --> 00:02:26,206
about 35,000 won lands in your account almost every business
day. That's around thirty dollars, every day.

31
00:02:26,756 --> 00:02:29,890
Step three. After about 166 payments,

32
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:34,590
roughly eight months, you've received
about 5.8 million won.

33
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:38,225
Your money back, plus 1.4 million won on
top.

34
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:44,683
The court that later tried the group's number two described
the promise as a fixed 35 percent a year.

35
00:02:44,683 --> 00:02:49,528
Run the numbers on eight months, and it
works out closer to 48 percent annualized.

36
00:02:49,528 --> 00:02:53,428
A normal Korean bank deposit at the time
paid around five percent.

37
00:02:54,428 --> 00:02:59,153
Korea actually has a word, and a whole
law, for this kind of business.

38
00:02:59,153 --> 00:03:03,135
It's called yusa-susin. Roughly:
unlicensed deposit-taking.

39
00:03:03,135 --> 00:03:07,354
It means collecting money from the public
and promising fixed returns,

40
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:11,572
without being a bank, and without any
license to do it.

41
00:03:11,822 --> 00:03:17,677
It's illegal in Korea for one simple reason. It is
the classic shape of a Ponzi scheme.

42
00:03:18,877 --> 00:03:21,775
The daily payment was the hook. The ladder
was the engine.

43
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:28,005
According to a December 2008 report by the
Korean news magazine Sisa IN,

44
00:03:28,005 --> 00:03:36,286
investors were organized into ranks, like a company. Department head.
Bureau chief. Division head. Group leader. Executive director.

45
00:03:36,836 --> 00:03:41,303
To become a department head, the report
said, you had to run two

46
00:03:41,303 --> 00:03:46,114
lines of investors below you, and each
line had to bring in twelve machines.

47
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:50,687
Twenty-four machines. Around 100 million
won of other people's money.

48
00:03:50,937 --> 00:03:54,148
Only then did the company pay you a salary
and bonuses.

49
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:59,811
To climb to the next rank, the requirement
reportedly jumped to 280 machines.

50
00:03:59,811 --> 00:04:02,400
Think about what that does to a person.

51
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:08,823
You are no longer just an investor. You are a
recruiter. Your income depends on your sister,

52
00:04:08,823 --> 00:04:14,270
your old classmates, the women from your church, the
man who runs the shop downstairs.

53
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,103
And the system had a second trick.

54
00:04:17,103 --> 00:04:21,939
According to the same report, the
operation kept changing its name. BMC.

55
00:04:21,939 --> 00:04:28,520
Then a series of new company names, one after another,
with different brands for different regions.

56
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:32,969
Prosecutors later found that the group ran
around twenty companies in total.

57
00:04:32,969 --> 00:04:36,781
When one name drew attention, the money
simply moved to the next one.

58
00:04:37,331 --> 00:04:40,640
But there was a basic problem underneath
all of it.

59
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,663
Machines rented to saunas could never earn
that kind of money.

60
00:04:44,663 --> 00:04:48,524
According to prosecutors, the "rental
income" being paid out

61
00:04:48,524 --> 00:04:52,385
was largely new investors' money, recycled
to earlier ones.

62
00:04:52,635 --> 00:04:55,619
When the medical device story wore thin,

63
00:04:55,619 --> 00:04:59,882
the pitch reportedly shifted to real
estate and redevelopment projects.

64
00:04:59,882 --> 00:05:02,493
The product changed. The promise didn't.

65
00:05:03,693 --> 00:05:05,587
The money did arrive. At first.

66
00:05:05,587 --> 00:05:09,252
Early investors were paid on time. They
told friends. They

67
00:05:09,252 --> 00:05:12,918
told family. And they earned commissions
for bringing them in.

68
00:05:13,468 --> 00:05:18,663
From Daegu, the network spread down to
Busan and the southern coast,

69
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:23,425
up through the Chungcheong provinces, and
into Seoul, Gyeonggi and Incheon.

70
00:05:23,425 --> 00:05:29,413
By late 2008, Korean media were counting around
fifty sales centers across the country.

71
00:05:29,963 --> 00:05:31,260
So who signed up?

72
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:34,311
Not bankers. Not professional investors.

73
00:05:34,561 --> 00:05:40,722
In the Seoul area, the same 2008 report
said, about seven in ten investors were

74
00:05:40,722 --> 00:05:46,062
homemakers in their forties and fifties. Later
reporting described large numbers of retirees,

75
00:05:46,062 --> 00:05:53,456
people who had lost jobs, and families still recovering from
the Asian financial crisis of the late 1990s.

76
00:05:53,706 --> 00:05:58,854
Some reportedly put in their retirement
money. Some borrowed to invest more.

77
00:05:59,854 --> 00:06:00,495
Why them?

78
00:06:00,495 --> 00:06:06,908
Think about where a middle-aged homemaker or a new
retiree stood in Korea in the mid-2000s.

79
00:06:06,908 --> 00:06:11,709
Interest rates were low. Pensions were
thin. Many had a lump sum,

80
00:06:11,709 --> 00:06:17,311
a severance payment or a matured insurance
policy, and nowhere obvious to put it.

81
00:06:17,561 --> 00:06:22,766
And the person making the pitch was rarely
a stranger. It was a

82
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:28,371
friend from the neighborhood, or a relative, someone
you had trusted for twenty years.

83
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:35,322
You put in your money. And then, every single day, a
payment lands in your account. Like clockwork.

84
00:06:35,322 --> 00:06:39,202
After a few weeks, the doubts fade. After
a few months,

85
00:06:39,202 --> 00:06:42,377
you're the one telling your sister she's
missing out.

86
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:47,929
That daily payment wasn't just a return.
It was proof. Or it looked like proof.

87
00:06:49,129 --> 00:06:51,588
Here's what makes this story hard to
watch.

88
00:06:51,588 --> 00:06:56,276
The scheme did not hide in the dark for four
years. The warnings are on the record.

89
00:06:56,826 --> 00:07:03,530
According to police and prosecutors, police began
looking into the group as early as 2006.

90
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:07,260
In May 2007, a police station in Miryang,

91
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:13,322
in South Gyeongsang Province, opened a formal
investigation into companies linked to Cho.

92
00:07:13,872 --> 00:07:19,080
And then something happened that a court
would later describe in detail.

93
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:24,007
In August 2007, Kang Tae-yong, Cho's
right-hand man, handed fifty million won,

94
00:07:24,007 --> 00:07:31,809
twice, to a police sergeant at the Daegu police agency. A
sergeant who was working on the Cho case.

95
00:07:32,059 --> 00:07:35,331
A district court later found that the
sergeant,

96
00:07:35,331 --> 00:07:40,240
in return, dragged out the investigation and gave the
group "investigative convenience."

97
00:07:40,490 --> 00:07:44,128
Prosecutors also alleged that, when police
asked for transaction records,

98
00:07:44,128 --> 00:07:50,677
the group handed over records from an old, defunct company
instead of the one actually taking the money.

99
00:07:51,227 --> 00:07:54,861
In April 2008, according to Sisa IN,

100
00:07:54,861 --> 00:08:03,166
Korea's financial regulator, the Financial Supervisory Service, flagged
two of the group's companies for illegal deposit-taking,

101
00:08:03,166 --> 00:08:07,319
and notified police in Daegu, Busan and
Incheon.

102
00:08:07,569 --> 00:08:12,043
In September 2008, victims began filing
complaints.

103
00:08:12,043 --> 00:08:14,236
By then, it was too late.

104
00:08:15,436 --> 00:08:21,064
In the autumn of 2008, the global financial crisis
was shaking markets everywhere.

105
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:24,399
In Korea, investigators were finally
closing in.

106
00:08:24,399 --> 00:08:28,984
In October 2008 the payments stopped, and
the companies collapsed.

107
00:08:29,234 --> 00:08:31,601
According to Korean reporting at the time,

108
00:08:31,601 --> 00:08:37,010
the group's central computer systems were wiped and the
accounts were emptied before police got there.

109
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,571
Cho was put on the wanted list.

110
00:08:39,571 --> 00:08:43,140
Some victims reportedly photocopied the
wanted posters themselves

111
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:46,708
and carried them to ports around the
country.

112
00:08:46,958 --> 00:08:49,671
But according to investigators, on
December 9th,

113
00:08:49,671 --> 00:08:55,871
2008, he boarded a fishing boat from a
small port in Taean, on the west coast.

114
00:08:55,871 --> 00:09:00,822
At sea, he reportedly switched to another
vessel and crossed to China.

115
00:09:01,372 --> 00:09:06,785
His right-hand man, Kang Tae-yong, had
already fled to China the month before.

116
00:09:06,785 --> 00:09:13,239
Kang was the group's administrative vice president. He
ran the money and the computer systems.

117
00:09:13,789 --> 00:09:19,712
Years later, prosecutors and the courts put a
number on what the group had collected.

118
00:09:19,712 --> 00:09:23,097
About 5 trillion won. Roughly 4 billion
dollars.

119
00:09:23,097 --> 00:09:24,930
From around 70,000 people.

120
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:30,744
That 5 trillion figure counts money that
went round and round,

121
00:09:30,744 --> 00:09:33,136
as people reinvested their "profits."

122
00:09:33,136 --> 00:09:35,260
Prosecutors estimated the real,

123
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:41,102
unrecovered losses at about 840 billion
won. Around 700 million dollars.

124
00:09:41,352 --> 00:09:45,752
And they found that the group had stashed
away about 290 billion won.

125
00:09:45,752 --> 00:09:50,980
Korean media started calling it the biggest fraud
since the founding of the nation.

126
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:55,003
Before we go on, keep two things in mind.

127
00:09:55,003 --> 00:10:01,342
One: a cheque, handed over in a hotel coffee shop,
to a man whose job was to catch Cho.

128
00:10:01,342 --> 00:10:04,384
Two: a single piece of paper from China,

129
00:10:04,384 --> 00:10:08,567
dated ten days before the death it was
supposed to record.

130
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:15,239
The first explains how he got away. The second is why so
many people still don't believe he's gone.

131
00:10:16,439 --> 00:10:20,755
Here's the part that turned a fraud case
into a national scandal.

132
00:10:20,755 --> 00:10:26,068
How did one man run a scheme this big, for
this long, and then simply sail away?

133
00:10:27,068 --> 00:10:31,455
We've already met the first one. The
police sergeant on the case.

134
00:10:31,455 --> 00:10:36,921
The court found he took 200 million won
from Kang in total. Two payments in 2007.

135
00:10:36,921 --> 00:10:43,805
And a third, on October 31st, 2008, at a hotel in Daegu,
while the group's offices were being searched.

136
00:10:44,055 --> 00:10:47,108
A district court sentenced him to eight
years in prison.

137
00:10:47,658 --> 00:10:49,439
The second was more senior.

138
00:10:49,439 --> 00:10:55,046
In October 2008, he headed the violent-crime unit at
the Daegu regional police agency.

139
00:10:55,296 --> 00:11:02,302
According to the courts, on October 30th, 2008, he met
Cho in person, in the coffee shop of

140
00:11:02,302 --> 00:11:09,308
a hotel in Daegu, and received 900 million won as a bank
cheque. That's around 800 thousand dollars.

141
00:11:09,558 --> 00:11:14,286
The courts found that he took the money in
return for investigation information.

142
00:11:14,286 --> 00:11:21,260
According to reporting on the ruling, he sat face to face with a man
his own agency was hunting, and let him walk away.

143
00:11:21,510 --> 00:11:24,350
He was sentenced to ten years at first,

144
00:11:24,350 --> 00:11:28,964
reduced to nine on appeal. The Supreme
Court confirmed it in December 2016.

145
00:11:29,514 --> 00:11:30,688
It went further.

146
00:11:30,688 --> 00:11:36,801
An official at the Daegu prosecutors' office, who
had worked on the Cho case itself,

147
00:11:36,801 --> 00:11:41,692
was convicted of taking bribes over five
years, from 2008 to 2013.

148
00:11:41,942 --> 00:11:47,654
Most of it, the court found, came from a businessman who
was managing the group's hidden money.

149
00:11:47,654 --> 00:11:53,002
In return, the official passed on prosecution
information and helped bury investigations.

150
00:11:53,252 --> 00:11:58,692
His sentence: nine years in prison and a
1.4 billion won fine,

151
00:11:58,692 --> 00:12:02,319
confirmed by the Supreme Court in October
2016.

152
00:12:02,869 --> 00:12:06,236
By the time the investigation was done,

153
00:12:06,236 --> 00:12:11,047
prosecutors said eight police and prosecution
officials had faced punishment.

154
00:12:11,047 --> 00:12:13,937
For the victims, this changed everything.

155
00:12:13,937 --> 00:12:19,436
The people who were supposed to catch him,
some of them, had been paid by him.

156
00:12:20,636 --> 00:12:21,982
So where was Cho?

157
00:12:21,982 --> 00:12:28,869
According to investigators, he moved around China,
reportedly living under another name.

158
00:12:28,869 --> 00:12:33,144
Then, in May 2012, Korean police made an
announcement.

159
00:12:33,694 --> 00:12:40,089
Cho Hee-pal, they said, had died in China in
December 2011, of an acute heart attack.

160
00:12:40,089 --> 00:12:45,019
Police said they had found Chinese
emergency-care records and a

161
00:12:45,019 --> 00:12:50,443
death certificate while searching the
homes of his family and associates.

162
00:12:50,993 --> 00:12:55,831
They pointed to a video of his funeral,
reportedly filmed by family members.

163
00:12:55,831 --> 00:13:01,065
According to Korean reporting at the time,
he had been cremated in China within days,

164
00:13:01,065 --> 00:13:05,253
and his ashes brought back to Korea before
the end of December.

165
00:13:05,503 --> 00:13:08,958
And Korean authorities only found out
about it five months later.

166
00:13:09,958 --> 00:13:11,471
The public didn't buy it.

167
00:13:11,471 --> 00:13:14,013
Cremation meant there was no body to test.

168
00:13:14,013 --> 00:13:20,307
Even the police kept him on the wanted list, saying the death
had not been confirmed one hundred percent.

169
00:13:20,857 --> 00:13:22,895
Remember that piece of paper?

170
00:13:22,895 --> 00:13:27,593
In 2015, victims' groups pointed to a
Chinese cremation document that,

171
00:13:27,593 --> 00:13:33,999
according to Korean reporting, was issued ten days
before the date of death it recorded.

172
00:13:34,249 --> 00:13:38,120
They also noted that early reports and
later official accounts

173
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:41,990
named different Chinese cities as the
place where he died.

174
00:13:41,990 --> 00:13:46,408
A man who said he was a former aide
reportedly told Korean media he

175
00:13:46,408 --> 00:13:50,827
had spoken with Cho on the phone after the
date of his supposed death.

176
00:13:51,077 --> 00:13:57,503
There were rumors of plastic surgery, of a new name, of
sightings in China and even back in Korea.

177
00:13:58,053 --> 00:14:03,350
None of those rumors were ever proven. But
the questions were understandable.

178
00:14:03,350 --> 00:14:08,852
After all, this was a case where officials
had already been caught taking money.

179
00:14:08,852 --> 00:14:15,112
In 2015, Korean TV investigative programs took the
story up again, and public pressure grew.

180
00:14:15,662 --> 00:14:17,904
Then, on October 10th, 2015,

181
00:14:17,904 --> 00:14:23,735
Chinese police arrested Kang Tae-yong, the
right-hand man, in the city of Wuxi.

182
00:14:23,735 --> 00:14:27,130
On December 16th, he was flown back to
Korea.

183
00:14:27,130 --> 00:14:33,770
Arriving at the prosecutors' office in Daegu, he was
asked by reporters if Cho was alive.

184
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:36,609
He said he had seen Cho dead with his own
eyes.

185
00:14:37,159 --> 00:14:43,803
In June 2016, after a two-year reinvestigation,
prosecutors announced their conclusion.

186
00:14:43,803 --> 00:14:49,594
They said Cho collapsed after a night of
drinking at a hotel in Weihai,

187
00:14:49,594 --> 00:14:56,626
in China's Shandong Province, and died at a hospital in
the early hours of December 19th, 2011.

188
00:14:56,876 --> 00:15:03,759
They said they had consistent testimony from people who were
with him, and from fourteen people at the funeral.

189
00:15:03,759 --> 00:15:09,703
They said a DNA test on hair matched Cho, and that the
funeral video showed no signs of editing.

190
00:15:09,953 --> 00:15:12,940
They said they had found no evidence that
he was alive.

191
00:15:13,490 --> 00:15:18,949
Because he was dead, they said, he could not be
charged. The case against him was closed.

192
00:15:18,949 --> 00:15:23,911
Some victims' groups rejected that conclusion and
said they would keep searching.

193
00:15:24,161 --> 00:15:30,028
They argued that a strand of hair proves who it came from,
not whether that person is still alive.

194
00:15:31,028 --> 00:15:32,636
So, is Cho Hee-pal dead?

195
00:15:32,636 --> 00:15:36,053
The official answer from Korean
authorities is yes.

196
00:15:36,053 --> 00:15:42,017
The doubts have never been proven. But for many
victims, they have never gone away either.

197
00:15:43,217 --> 00:15:49,751
With Cho officially dead, Kang Tae-yong became the
highest-ranking member of the group ever to stand trial.

198
00:15:49,751 --> 00:15:52,032
Prosecutors asked for life in prison.

199
00:15:52,582 --> 00:15:56,459
In January 2017, a Daegu court found that,

200
00:15:56,459 --> 00:16:04,699
from June 2006 to October 2008, together with Cho, he
had collected around 5 trillion won from

201
00:16:04,699 --> 00:16:11,000
roughly 70,000 people through unlicensed
deposit-taking, promising a fixed 35 percent a year.

202
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:16,085
It convicted him of fraud, embezzlement,
bribery, and hiding criminal proceeds.

203
00:16:16,085 --> 00:16:22,145
The court also found that he had paid the police sergeant
on the case for investigation information.

204
00:16:22,395 --> 00:16:26,552
And it found that, in China, he had worked
with organized-crime

205
00:16:26,552 --> 00:16:30,709
figures to try to collect money he
believed he was owed.

206
00:16:30,709 --> 00:16:37,346
The judges called it a property crime almost without
precedent, one that had destroyed families.

207
00:16:37,346 --> 00:16:39,372
He was sentenced to 22 years.

208
00:16:39,922 --> 00:16:43,782
On appeal, his lawyers argued the sentence
was too harsh,

209
00:16:43,782 --> 00:16:47,641
and that some of the charges were really
Cho's doing.

210
00:16:47,641 --> 00:16:53,536
The appeal court kept the 22 years. In November
2017, the Supreme Court confirmed it.

211
00:16:54,086 --> 00:16:57,905
Prosecutors charged around seventy people
in total over the scheme.

212
00:16:57,905 --> 00:17:01,953
But the man whose name was on the whole
thing would never face a judge.

213
00:17:03,153 --> 00:17:07,669
That gap, a crime with no trial for the
man at the top,

214
00:17:07,669 --> 00:17:12,532
is part of why the story escaped the
courtroom and moved into pop culture.

215
00:17:12,782 --> 00:17:18,742
In December 2016, the Korean crime film "Master" opened in
theaters. It's about a smooth-talking chairman

216
00:17:18,742 --> 00:17:24,703
who runs a giant investment fraud, and
tries to fake his way out of the country.

217
00:17:24,703 --> 00:17:28,396
Its director said the villain was modeled
on Cho Hee-pal.

218
00:17:28,646 --> 00:17:32,025
More than seven million people saw it in
Korean cinemas.

219
00:17:32,575 --> 00:17:35,791
And the phrase Korean media had used,

220
00:17:35,791 --> 00:17:40,385
"the biggest fraud since the founding of
the nation," stuck.

221
00:17:40,385 --> 00:17:46,906
For a whole generation, Cho's name became
shorthand for a con man who never paid.

222
00:17:48,106 --> 00:17:50,214
But what about the victims?

223
00:17:50,214 --> 00:17:55,990
Prosecutors said about 95 billion won had
been tracked down and recovered.

224
00:17:55,990 --> 00:17:59,425
Against the losses, that's a small
fraction.

225
00:17:59,425 --> 00:18:06,294
Spread across 70,000 people, it's roughly 1.4 million
won each. About a thousand dollars.

226
00:18:06,844 --> 00:18:09,737
And getting even that has taken years.

227
00:18:09,737 --> 00:18:16,316
About 71 billion won of the criminal proceeds was
deposited with a court in Daegu,

228
00:18:16,316 --> 00:18:20,702
split into four separate cases, to be
divided among creditors.

229
00:18:20,952 --> 00:18:24,320
The first payout came in December 2017:

230
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:28,169
32 billion won, to more than 17,000
creditors.

231
00:18:28,169 --> 00:18:33,216
Then some victims challenged who should
count as a creditor,

232
00:18:33,216 --> 00:18:37,254
and how much each should get. Everything
froze.

233
00:18:37,254 --> 00:18:40,820
Those lawsuits only ended in October 2025.

234
00:18:41,370 --> 00:18:46,203
On June 24th, 2026, the court in Daegu
restarted the payouts:

235
00:18:46,203 --> 00:18:53,672
another 32 billion won, to more than 22,000 creditors,
with money due to go out from July.

236
00:18:53,672 --> 00:19:00,027
Two smaller pots, of 5 billion and 2
billion won, are still waiting for dates.

237
00:19:00,277 --> 00:19:04,712
Korean media reported that more than
20,000 victims were represented by

238
00:19:04,712 --> 00:19:09,147
only around a hundred appointed
representatives, and that many had moved,

239
00:19:09,147 --> 00:19:12,648
changed numbers, or simply lost contact.
The court said

240
00:19:12,648 --> 00:19:16,539
it had to track people down through local
administrative offices.

241
00:19:16,789 --> 00:19:20,418
Some, after eighteen years, will never see
the money at all.

242
00:19:21,418 --> 00:19:24,715
For many families, the damage was never
just financial.

243
00:19:24,715 --> 00:19:29,510
Korean media reported that some victims
lost homes, marriages, and their health.

244
00:19:29,510 --> 00:19:32,087
Many have spent nearly two decades
waiting.

245
00:19:33,287 --> 00:19:35,301
Here's the uncomfortable part.

246
00:19:35,301 --> 00:19:39,395
Cho's scheme collapsed in 2008. The idea
behind it never did.

247
00:19:39,945 --> 00:19:44,964
In February 2025, Korea's financial
regulator said it had received

248
00:19:44,964 --> 00:19:48,477
410 reports about unlicensed
deposit-taking in 2024,

249
00:19:48,477 --> 00:19:55,001
up a quarter from the year before. It
referred 35 companies to police.

250
00:19:55,251 --> 00:20:02,236
The packaging has changed. Instead of massage chairs, it's
new technology, crypto assets, real estate.

251
00:20:02,236 --> 00:20:03,758
But look at the shape.

252
00:20:04,308 --> 00:20:09,568
One. A fixed, guaranteed return, far above
what any bank pays.

253
00:20:09,568 --> 00:20:11,846
Two. Payments that come often,

254
00:20:11,846 --> 00:20:18,223
daily or weekly, so the scheme feels real
long before it can be checked.

255
00:20:18,223 --> 00:20:21,914
Three. A product story you can't easily
verify.

256
00:20:21,914 --> 00:20:25,604
A machine in a sauna you'll never visit.

257
00:20:25,854 --> 00:20:31,509
Four. Bonuses for bringing in others, so
victims become recruiters.

258
00:20:31,509 --> 00:20:35,687
And five. Time. Every month that nobody
stops it,

259
00:20:35,687 --> 00:20:41,723
the hole gets deeper, and the people
inside it get more people involved.

260
00:20:42,723 --> 00:20:46,102
None of this is unique to Korea. Versions
of the

261
00:20:46,102 --> 00:20:49,818
same shape have turned up across Asia and
around the world.

262
00:20:49,818 --> 00:20:56,581
That's not investment advice. It's a pattern. And patterns
are the one thing a Ponzi scheme can't hide.

263
00:20:57,781 --> 00:21:01,787
Officially, the story ended in a hotel
room in China in 2011.

264
00:21:01,787 --> 00:21:06,122
For many of the people who lost
everything, it never ended at all.

265
00:21:07,122 --> 00:21:10,612
The scheme didn't run on machines. It ran
on trust.

266
00:21:10,612 --> 00:21:15,060
Trust in a daily payment. Trust in the
friend who brought you in.

267
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:19,782
And trust that if something was wrong, the
authorities would stop it.

268
00:21:20,332 --> 00:21:24,866
So here's the question to ask, every time:
if a business pays you

269
00:21:24,866 --> 00:21:29,400
more than any bank ever could, where is
that money really coming from?

270
00:21:29,950 --> 00:21:32,048
Next time: another country,

271
00:21:32,048 --> 00:21:37,818
another promise that sounded safe. And
another fortune that simply disappeared.

272
00:21:38,368 --> 00:21:40,172
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

273
00:21:40,172 --> 00:21:45,351
And tell me in the comments: have you ever been
offered a deal that paid every single day?

